  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data exploration**\nThe `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. The fields I used are `response` (string, with a `response.keyword` subfield), `clientip`, `url.keyword`, `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `bytes`, and `extension`.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-533fe04f Web logs overview\"**\nIt has 6 panels, with a default time range of the last 7 days:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram)\n- Requests by response code (bar, top 10)\n- Unique client IPs (metric)\n- Top requested URLs (table, top 10)\n- 5xx responses (metric, ES|QL: `WHERE response.keyword LIKE \"5*\"`)\n\nURL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-533fe04f-web-logs-overview-b692e33dc11f\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-533fe04f 5xx spike\"**\n- Type: `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`, id `ea14ccf7-28e1-43fc-9002-b2f6be9612ac`\n- Runs every 1m and counts documents on `kibana_sample_data_logs` whose `response.keyword` matches `5*`, using a 5-minute window on `timestamp`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Status: enabled. It shows `pending` because it hasn't run yet.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- I used a wildcard `5*` match on the string `response.keyword`, because the data view's filters only support equality.\n- I created the rule disabled, then enabled it after checking that alerting health was `ok`.\n\n**Not verified**\nI couldn't query the data directly. Kibana's ES|QL endpoint returned 404, and Elasticsearch on port 9200 wasn't reachable. So I haven't confirmed that the panels show data or that the 5xx count is above or below the threshold. The dashboard and rule definitions were confirmed through the API.",
